          <h2 class="article-title">
            The Y Combinator (YC) Pitch Deck Framework
          </h2>

          <p class="article-lead">
            Y Combinator’s (YC) pitch deck framework is specifically tailored for the seed stage. Because early-stage startups typically lack years of financial data or comprehensive market share, YC emphasizes clarity, concision, and a compelling narrative over exhaustive details or world philosophy.
          </p>

          <div class="article-meta-bar">
            <span class="meta-item"><strong>Reading time:</strong> 8 min read</span>
            <span class="meta-divider">&bull;</span>
            <span class="meta-item"><strong>Accelerator:</strong> Y Combinator</span>
            <span class="meta-divider">&bull;</span>
            <span class="meta-item"><strong>Format:</strong> 9 Slides + Kevin Hale Philosophy</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- YC Core Philosophy: Legible, Simple, Obvious -->
        <section class="yc-philosophy-section">
          <div class="section-tag-row">
            <span class="section-badge-pill">CORE PRINCIPLE</span>
            <span class="section-tag-name">KEVIN HALE'S THREE LAWS</span>
          </div>

          <h3 class="slide-heading">The Core Philosophy: "Legible, Simple, Obvious"</h3>

          <p class="slide-intro">
            Kevin Hale, a former YC partner, famously codified the accelerator's visual and structural rules for pitching into three words:
          </p>

          <div class="philosophy-cards-grid">
            <div class="philosophy-card">
              <div class="ph-card-icon">01</div>
              <h4 class="ph-card-title">Legible</h4>
              <p class="ph-card-desc">
                If investors can't read it, they won't understand it. Use large, bold typography with strong contrast. Avoid faint greys or tiny font sizes.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="philosophy-card">
              <div class="ph-card-icon">02</div>
              <h4 class="ph-card-title">Simple</h4>
              <p class="ph-card-desc">
                A simple slide expresses exactly one idea. Do not crowd slides with multiple concepts. YC strongly advises against complex diagrams—calling them <em>"mazes for ideas"</em> that waste investors' time.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="philosophy-card">
              <div class="ph-card-icon">03</div>
              <h4 class="ph-card-title">Obvious</h4>
              <p class="ph-card-desc">
                Slides must be understood at a glance. YC recommends the <strong>"stranger test"</strong>: if you show a slide to a stranger and they cannot immediately grasp the main point, the slide fails.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

