        <!-- YC 9-Slide Overview Cards Grid -->
        <section class="summary-cards-section">
          <div class="summary-cards-grid">
            {ycSlides.map((slide) => (
              <a href={`#yc-slide-${parseInt(slide.num)}`} class="summary-card">
                <div class="card-num-row">
                  <span class="slide-badge">SLIDE {slide.num}</span>
                  <span class="slide-question">{slide.question}</span>
                </div>
                <h3 class="card-slide-title">{slide.title}</h3>
                <p class="card-slide-desc">{slide.desc}</p>
              </a>
            ))}
          </div>
        </section>

        <hr class="section-divider" />

        <!-- YC Slide Deep Dives (1 to 9) -->
        <div class="key-points-list">
          
          <!-- Slide 1: Title -->
          <div id="yc-slide-1" class="slide-deep-dive">
            <div class="slide-tag-row">
              <span class="slide-number-pill">01</span>
              <span class="slide-tag-title">YC SLIDE 1</span>
            </div>
            <h3 class="slide-heading">1. Title</h3>
            <p class="point-text">
              This slide should include your <strong>company name and a highly concise, one-line description</strong> of exactly what you do.
            </p>
          </div>

          <hr class="section-divider" />

          <!-- Slide 2: Problem -->
          <div id="yc-slide-2" class="slide-deep-dive">
            <div class="slide-tag-row">
              <span class="slide-number-pill">02</span>
              <span class="slide-tag-title">YC SLIDE 2</span>
            </div>
            <h3 class="slide-heading">2. Problem</h3>
            <p class="point-text">
              State the problem <strong>clearly and without jargon</strong>. You must explain how this problem specifically impacts real-world people or businesses.
            </p>
          </div>

          <hr class="section-divider" />

          <!-- Slide 3: Solution -->
          <div id="yc-slide-3" class="slide-deep-dive">
            <div class="slide-tag-row">
              <span class="slide-number-pill">03</span>
              <span class="slide-tag-title">YC SLIDE 3</span>
            </div>
            <h3 class="slide-heading">3. Solution</h3>
            <p class="point-text">
              Explain your product in <strong>as few words as possible</strong>. Rather than listing highly technical features, describe the concrete, immediate benefits your solution provides to the user.
            </p>
          </div>

          <hr class="section-divider" />

          <!-- Slide 4: Traction / Progress -->
          <div id="yc-slide-4" class="slide-deep-dive">
            <div class="slide-tag-row">
              <span class="slide-number-pill">04</span>
              <span class="slide-tag-title">YC SLIDE 4</span>
            </div>
            <h3 class="slide-heading">4. Traction / Progress</h3>
            <p class="point-text">
              If you have metrics, show them here. <strong>YC values the ratio of execution over time</strong>: they want to see how much you have accomplished relative to how long you have been working on the startup. Make the charts explicit and easy to read.
            </p>
            <div class="callout-tip">
              <strong>Execution Velocity:</strong> Investors are assessing the slope of your growth curve. Accomplishing $10K MRR in 2 months is vastly more impressive than $10K MRR after 3 years.
            </div>
          </div>

          <hr class="section-divider" />

          <!-- Slide 5: Unique Insight -->
          <div id="yc-slide-5" class="slide-deep-dive">
            <div class="slide-tag-row">
              <span class="slide-number-pill">05</span>
              <span class="slide-tag-title">YC SLIDE 5</span>
            </div>
            <h3 class="slide-heading">5. Unique Insight</h3>
            <p class="point-text">
              This is often the <strong>most critical slide in a YC deck</strong>. You must demonstrate an asymmetric understanding of the market by answering: <em>What do you know about this problem that everyone else doesn't?</em>
            </p>
            <p class="point-text">
              This insight should be derived from deep customer conversations or specific personal experience, not a generic complaint. For example, claiming <em>"email is broken"</em> is not an insight, but recognizing that <strong>"an inbox is a personal database where users should never have to delete anything"</strong> was the unique insight behind Gmail.
            </p>
            <div class="callout-conclusion">
              <strong>The Asymmetric Advantage:</strong> A unique insight proves that you have discovered a secret about customer behavior or market structure before the rest of the world caught on.
            </div>
          </div>

          <hr class="section-divider" />

          <!-- Slide 6: Business Model -->
          <div id="yc-slide-6" class="slide-deep-dive">
            <div class="slide-tag-row">
              <span class="slide-number-pill">06</span>
              <span class="slide-tag-title">YC SLIDE 6</span>
            </div>
            <h3 class="slide-heading">6. Business Model</h3>
            <p class="point-text">
              Explain how you make money. <strong>YC explicitly warns against offering a "potpourri" of business models</strong> (like trying to do subscriptions, ads, and virtual goods all at once). Pick the single most logical monetization strategy for your space and own it.
            </p>
          </div>

          <hr class="section-divider" />

          <!-- Slide 7: Market Size -->
          <div id="yc-slide-7" class="slide-deep-dive">
            <div class="slide-tag-row">
              <span class="slide-number-pill">07</span>
              <span class="slide-tag-title">YC SLIDE 7</span>
            </div>
            <h3 class="slide-heading">7. Market Size</h3>
            <p class="point-text">
              Show how big the opportunity is, but avoid lazy "top-down" estimates (e.g., <em>"It's a $4 trillion market"</em>). <strong>YC strongly prefers a "bottom-up" analysis</strong>, where you identify your specific, narrowed-down target customer base, determine how much they pay, and calculate the realistic market opportunity from there.
            </p>
          </div>

          <hr class="section-divider" />

          <!-- Slide 8: Team -->
          <div id="yc-slide-8" class="slide-deep-dive">
            <div class="slide-tag-row">
              <span class="slide-number-pill">08</span>
              <span class="slide-tag-title">YC SLIDE 8</span>
            </div>
            <h3 class="slide-heading">8. Team</h3>
            <p class="point-text">
              This slide should explain why your specific team is uniquely suited to tackle this exact problem. <strong>YC is adamant about one rule here: focus purely on the founders.</strong> Do not waste valuable space listing peripheral advisors.
            </p>
            <div class="callout-warning">
              <strong>YC Heuristic:</strong> Early stage VCs back founders, not advisors. Listing advisors suggests founders need external crutches to look legitimate.
            </div>
          </div>

          <hr class="section-divider" />

          <!-- Slide 9: Ask -->
          <div id="yc-slide-9" class="slide-deep-dive">
            <div class="slide-tag-row">
              <span class="slide-number-pill">09</span>
              <span class="slide-tag-title">YC SLIDE 9</span>
            </div>
            <h3 class="slide-heading">9. Ask</h3>
            <p class="point-text">
              Conclude by stating exactly how much money you are raising and what that capital will buy you. Specifically, <strong>YC wants founders to map out how this money will fund the company over the next year to hit the milestones necessary to become "Series A ready"</strong>.
            </p>
          </div>

        </div>

        <!-- Back to Top / Navigation -->
        <div class="article-footer-nav">
