      <!-- ARTICLE 6: The Get Backed narrative framework                             -->
      <!-- ========================================================================= -->
      <article id="article-6" class="article-block">
        <!-- Article 6 Header -->
        <div class="article-header">
          <div class="article-category-badge">
            <span class="badge-dot"></span>
            <span>ARTICLE 06 &bull; STORYTELLING &amp; RELATIONSHIPS &bull; GET BACKED</span>
          </div>

          <h2 class="article-title">
            The Get Backed Narrative Framework
          </h2>

          <p class="article-lead">
            The Get Backed narrative framework originates from the book <em>"Get Backed: Craft Your Story, Build the Perfect Pitch Deck, and Launch the Venture of Your Dreams,"</em> written by entrepreneurs Evan Baehr and Evan Loomis. Unlike other frameworks that focus purely on data and metrics, this methodology emphasizes that a pitch deck is fundamentally a storytelling artifact used to build genuine relationships.
          </p>

          <div class="article-meta-bar">
            <span class="meta-item"><strong>Reading time:</strong> 8 min read</span>
            <span class="meta-divider">&bull;</span>
            <span class="meta-item"><strong>Authors:</strong> Evan Baehr &amp; Evan Loomis</span>
            <span class="meta-divider">&bull;</span>
            <span class="meta-item"><strong>Empirical Base:</strong> 15 Decks Raising $150M+</span>
            <span class="meta-divider">&bull;</span>
            <span class="meta-item"><strong>Structure:</strong> 4 Core Narratives &bull; 10 Slides &bull; Friendship Loop</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Section 1: The Four Core Narratives -->
        <section class="narratives-section">
          <div class="section-tag-row">
            <span class="section-badge-pill">FOUNDATIONAL STORYTELLING</span>
            <span class="section-tag-name">THE FOUR CORE NARRATIVES</span>
          </div>

          <h3 class="slide-heading">1. The Four Core Narratives</h3>
          <p class="slide-intro">
            The framework suggests that a successful pitch weaves together four specific stories that every entrepreneur must know how to tell. Instead of just listing facts, founders should overlay these narrative arcs onto their slides:
          </p>

          <div class="narratives-cards-grid">
            {getBackedNarratives.map((item) => (
              <div class="narrative-card">
                <div class="narrative-tagline">{item.num} &bull; {item.tagline}</div>
                <h4 class="narrative-title">{item.title}</h4>
                <p class="narrative-desc">{item.desc}</p>
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>
        </section>

        <!-- Section 2: The Friendship Loop -->
        <section class="friendship-loop-section">
          <div class="section-tag-row">
            <span class="section-badge-pill">RELATIONSHIP CAPITAL</span>
            <span class="section-tag-name">DISCIPLINED ENGAGEMENT PROCESS</span>
          </div>

          <h3 class="slide-heading">2. The "Friendship Loop"</h3>
          <p class="slide-intro">
            Perhaps the most unique element of the Get Backed methodology is its focus on the <strong>"Friendship Loop."</strong> The authors argue that founders should not just ask for cash; they need to cultivate relationships first. The framework outlines a disciplined, four-step approach to engaging potential investors before the pitch deck is even presented:
          </p>

          <div class="friendship-loop-grid">
            {friendshipLoopSteps.map((step) => (
              <div class="friendship-step-card">
                <div class="friendship-step-header">
                  <span class="friendship-step-badge">STEP {step.step}</span>
                  <span class="friendship-step-action">{step.action}</span>
                </div>
                <h4 class="friendship-step-title">{step.title}</h4>
                <p class="friendship-step-desc">{step.desc}</p>
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>

          <div class="callout-conclusion">
            <strong>The Relationship Rule:</strong> Fundraising is not a transaction; it is an adoption. Investors back people they know, like, and trust. By walking through the Friendship Loop before presenting your deck, you enter the pitch room as a trusted partner rather than a desperate solicitor.
          </div>
        </section>

        <!-- Section 3: The 10 Essential Slides -->
        <section class="slides-overview-section">
          <div class="section-tag-row">
            <span class="section-badge-pill">DECK ARCHITECTURE</span>
            <span class="section-tag-name">15 DECKS &bull; $150M+ RAISED</span>
          </div>

          <h3 class="slide-heading">3. The 10 Essential Slides</h3>
          <p class="slide-intro">
            Baehr and Loomis analyzed 15 real-world pitch decks that successfully raised over $150 million to develop their required slide structure. Here is the recommended 10-slide sequence:
          </p>

          <!-- Quick Navigation Bar for Article 6 -->
          <nav class="toc-nav" aria-label="Get Backed slide quick links">
            <span class="toc-label">Jump to slide:</span>
            <div class="toc-pills">
              {getBackedSlides.map((slide) => (
